/2}\bigr)}{\sctrl}.
\end{equation}
The factor $b$ is the exact fibre multiplicity.

Now let $h\in S_{\mathrm{ext}}$.  There is a label $m=m(h)$ with
\[
 |m|\le C/\sctrl\le N,
 \qquad h\equiv m\pmod p\quad(p\in\Bset).
\]
Because $h\notin S_M$, it is not congruent to $m$ modulo $L$.  If it agreed
with $m$ modulo every prime divisor of squarefree $b$, then it would agree
modulo $b$.  Together with the block congruences and coprimality of $b$ and
$P$, CRT would give agreement modulo $L$, a contradiction.  Hence one may
choose a prime
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mismatch-prime}
 r(h)\mid b,
 \qquad h\not\equiv m(h)\pmod{r(h)}.
\end{equation}
This is the second independent use of squarefreeness.

After $N$ and the damping target are fixed, choose one common set $\mathcal G$
of exactly $G$ primes in the designated high block, all outside the prime
support of $b$, with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:high-prime-separation}
 2N<s\qquad(s\in\mathcal G).
\end{equation}
The density clause of $\mathbf{AI}$ supplies this set at the final scale.
Include in $E$ every gadget denominator
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gadget-family}
 rs\qquad(r\mid b\text{ prime},\ s\in\mathcal G).
\end{equation}
Unique factorization recovers both primes, so all gadget products are distinct;
they cannot collide with two-block-prime products because block primes lie
outside the prime support of $b$.

Fix $h\in S_{\mathrm{ext}}$, write $m=m(h)$ and $r=r(h)$, and take
$s\in\mathcal G$.  Since $h\equiv m\pmod s$, write $h=m+js$.  The mismatch
modulo $r$ says $j\not\equiv0\pmod r$.  Therefore
\[
 \normdist{j/r}\ge\frac1r,
 \qquad
 \left|\frac{m}{rs}\right|<\frac1{2r},
\]
and the triangle inequality on $\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$ gives
\[
 \normdist{h/(rs)}\ge\frac1{2r}.
\]
The one-factor identity used above now yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gadget-factor}
 \left|(1-\theta)+\theta e^{2\pi ih/(rs)}\right|
 \le\sqrt{1-\frac8{9r^2}}
 \le\sqrt{1-\frac8{9b^2}}=:\beta_b<1.
\end{equation}
The $G$ factors are distinct, so $|F(h)|\le\beta_b^G$.
There are at most $2N+1$ labels and at most $b$ frequencies in each block
fibre; hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:extra-count}
 |S_{\mathrm{ext}}|\le b(2N+1),
 \qquad
 \sum_{h\in S_{\mathrm{ext}}}|F(h)|
 \le b(2N+1)\beta_b^G.
\end{equation}

\section{Terminal parameters and positivity}\label{sec:terminal}

Set $K_\sigma=501$.  The constants are chosen in the strict dependency order
\begin{equation}\label{eq:parameter-order}
 \eta\longrightarrow C\longrightarrow D_{\mathrm{mp}}
 \longrightarrow G\longrightarrow k_0.
\end{equation}
More precisely:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Step \arabic*.}]
\item Set
\begin{equation}\label{eq:eta-choice}
 \eta=\frac{c_3}{4K_\sigma b}
 =\frac{c_3}{2004b}.
\end{equation}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:global-partition} with $c=16/9$, obtaining
$C_{\mathrm{tail}}$ and a scale threshold.
\item Choose $C\ge3$ so large that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gaussian-quarter}
 bC_{\mathrm{tail}}e^{-(16/9)C^2/2}
 <\frac{c_3}{4K_\sigma}.
\end{equation}
\item Set
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Dmp}
 D_{\mathrm{mp}}
 =\frac{c_3}{4K_\sigma b(2C+3)}
 =\frac{c_3}{2004b(2C+3)},
\end{equation}
and choose $G$ so that $\beta_b^G\le D_{\mathrm{mp}}$.
\item Only now choose $k_0$ above the finite maximum of: the common analytic
threshold $K_{\mathrm{AI}}$; the control-load and deviation thresholds; the
cold-block, level-set, localization, and Laplace thresholds; the availability
and size of the $G$ common gadget primes; avoidance of $T$; the pair-pool and
mass-batch thresholds; the variance comparison; period and main-window
geometry; the Taylor disk and cubic remainder; and the remaining finite
cardinality and inverse-square certificates.
\end{enumerate}
No later choice changes $\eta,C,D_{\mathrm{mp}}$, or $G$.  Since
$N=\lceil C/\sctrl\rceil$ and eventually $\sctrl\le1$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:N-bound}
 (2N+1)\sctrl\le2C+3.
\end{equation}
Multiplying the minor estimates by $\sctrl$, the three contributions satisfy
\begin{align}
 b\eta&=\frac{c_3}{4K_\sigma},\label{eq:quarter-one}\\
 bC_{\mathrm{tail}}e^{-(16/9)C^2/2}
 &<\frac{c_3}{4K_\sigma},\label{eq:quarter-two}\\
 b(2N+1)\sctrl D_{\mathrm{mp}}
 &\le\frac{c_3}{4K_\sigma}.
 \label{eq:quarter-three}
\end{align}
Here $2004=4\cdot501$.  Therefore
\begin{equation}\label{eq:strict-minor}
 \sum_{h\in S_m}|F(h)|
 <\frac{3c_3}{4K_\sigma\sctrl}
 <\frac{c_3}{K_\sigma\sctrl}
 \le\frac{c_3}{\sE}.
\end{equation}
The last inequality uses $\sE\le K_\sigma\sctrl$.  By
\eqref{eq:main-lower}, the real main sum is at least $c_3/\sE$, so
Lemma~\ref{lem:spectral-positivity} gives a subset $A\subseteq E$ of positive
weight satisfying the required congruence.  The load bound
\eqref{eq:theta-window} converts it to
\[
 \sum_{e\in A}\frac1e=\frac1b.
\]
All denominators are distinct squarefree semiprimes and avoid $T$.  This proves
Theorem~\ref{thm:structural-construction}; Lemmas~\ref{lem:small-b} and
\ref{lem:numerator-reduction} prove the sufficiency direction of
Theorem~\ref{thm:headline}.

\section{Finite-set bridge to the public tuple}\label{sec:tuple}

The construction produces a nonempty finite set $S$: it cannot be empty because
its reciprocal sum is the positive rational $q$.  Write $|S|=m+1$ and enumerate
it increasingly by
\[
 g:\operatorname{Fin}(m+1)\hookrightarrow\N.
\]
Define $n:\operatorname{Fin}(m+2)\to\N$ by
\[
 n(0)=1,
 \qquad n(j+1)=g(j).
\]
Every squarefree semiprime exceeds $1$, so the anchor is strictly below the
first genuine denominator, and $n$ is strictly increasing.  For every
noninitial index, Lemma~\ref{lem:semiprime-equivalence} gives
$\omega(n_i)=\Omega(n_i)=2$, while
\[
 q=\sum_{i=1}^{m+1}\frac1{n_i}.
\]
The dummy anchor $1$ is excluded from the sum.  This is a bridge from a finite
set to a finite strictly increasing tuple; no infinite sequence is asserted.

\section{Formal evidence and exact trust boundary}\label{sec:trust}

\subsection{Paper proof}
The sole load-bearing external analytic theorem in this article is PNT
\eqref{eq:PNT}.  The Abel bridge, common threshold, inclusive endpoint, and all
downstream human implications are displayed in the paper.  Long rational
rearrangements, threshold maxima, cast and floor discharge, aggregate Taylor
constants, inverse-square arithmetic, and the final numerical certificate are
transparently retained as named finite/kernel residuals in the immutable Lean
companion; they introduce no additional external premise.

\subsection{Immutable released evidence}
The archived formal evidence is
\[
 \texttt{v0.0.3@4582185de1e0e27416e9362e0cc7943c3d2fb4fe},
\]
cited as \cite{TangErdos306v003} and archived under DOI
\texttt{10.5281/zenodo.20767390}.  Its exact headline axiom audit is
\begin{gather*}
 \texttt{propext},\qquad \texttt{Classical.choice},\qquad
 \texttt{Quot.sound},\\
 \texttt{RosserSchoenfeld.rosser\_schoenfeld\_cor3},\\
 \texttt{RosserSchoenfeld.rosser\_schoenfeld\_thm5}.
\end{gather*}
The last two are the only nonstandard mathematical assumptions.  They are
identified with Rosser--Schoenfeld \cite{RosserSchoenfeld1962}, Corollary~3,
equation~(3.8), p.~69, and Theorem~5, equations~(3.17)--(3.18), p.~70.  The
first supplies the released dyadic-density theorem, with the explicit cutoff
$k\ge5$; the second supplies the released inclusive reciprocal window, with
$k_0\ge20$ and upper endpoint $2^{3k_0+1}$.  The PNT argument in this article
does not alter that release audit.

Human-visible symbol-by-symbol comparison of the two formal transcriptions
with the publisher scan on pp.~69--70 remains a separate source-verification
gate.  It is not represented here as complete.

\subsection{Development and assurance layers}
The proof-development checkpoint
\[
 \texttt{proof-development/e306-rigour-v1@2eda43717603877d4c5a879b3103f99e36d5e6d1}
\]
supplied the human expansions consumed in this revision.  It is an authorial
proof-development source, not independent formal authority.  The frozen
checkpoint
\[
 \texttt{codex/pushlinter@e55ef359a8b98525f0bac6c7a510fcad94469bff}
\]
is secondary read-only architecture and declaration context only; no premise
or proof status transfers from it.

Independent Review A accepted the PNT bridge subject to the common-threshold
and inclusive-window explicitness repairs now incorporated.  Independent
Review B found no material mathematical gap, unrecorded downstream theorem,
circular parameter dependence, or material theorem mismatch; it required the
bounded expansions now incorporated in this candidate.  The revised candidate
still awaits a final fixed-candidate mathematical, source, and package review.
It is not described as independently audited or submission-ready.

\section{Reproducibility, novelty, and disclosure}

A clean checkout of the immutable release can be checked with its pinned Lean
and Mathlib environment using
\begin{verbatim}
cd lean
lake exe cache get
lake build
lake env lean RequestProject/Audit.lean
\end{verbatim}
The final command prints the five-item audit above.  The release, rather than
this manuscript branch or either development checkpoint, is the stable
software citation.

The conservative novelty claim is that the archived software contains a
machine-checked proof of the stated Formal Conjectures formulation of Erd\H{o}s
Problem~306, conditional on the two named Rosser--Schoenfeld statements, and
that this article gives a PNT-first mathematical reconstruction of the proof
architecture.  No claim of peer review, final independent verification, or
publication acceptance is made.  The benchmark and historical problem sources
are \cite{FormalConjectures2026,ErdosGraham1980}.

The project used AI-assisted mathematical exploration, Lean development,
source organization, and manuscript drafting.  Responsibility for the
mathematical claims, source selection, and final submitted text remains with
the author.  Final disclosure and acknowledgement wording remain author
decisions.

